# pages: pairs of stanzas with theme, svg, point, key
P=[
((1,2),'ŚRĪNIVĀSA, DARK AS A RAIN CLOUD',lambda: VIS(110,0,.7)+'<circle cx="104" cy="80" r="5" fill="#e05a8a" stroke="#111"/>'+'<path d="M20 40 q10 -24 34 -18 q14 -18 36 -4" fill="#3b5a8a" stroke="#111"/>',
 'Govinda Nāmālu','<b>The opening four.</b> The chant begins exactly like the Suprabhātam and the Sahasranāma: with Śrīnivāsa, "where Śrī lives", and the Lord of the Veṅkaṭa hill. Then the two qualities devotees love most: <i>bhaktavatsala</i>, a mother\'s love for devotees, and <i>bhāgavatapriya</i>, dear to those who love Him.','The Lord of the hills, <b>who loves His devotees</b>.'),
((3,4),'THE BUTTER THIEF WHO PROTECTS THE GOOD',lambda: K(80,0,.7)+'<path d="M130 110 q-4 -24 16 -28 q20 4 16 28z" fill="#c0703a" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M134 84 q12 -8 24 0" fill="#fff" stroke="#111"/>'+lab(180,40,'ŚIṢṬA',9),
 '','<b>Child and protector.</b> Stanza 3 is little Kṛṣṇa of Gokula: Nanda\'s darling, stealer of butter, cowherd. Stanza 4 is the protector: He destroys the wicked, removes misfortune and hardship, and guards the good. The same Lord, playful and powerful.','The playful child <b>is also the great protector</b>.'),
((5,6),'THE DIAMOND CROWN AND RĀMA',lambda: VIS(60,0,.55)+'<path d="M44 20 l6 -10 l6 8 l6 -8 l6 10z" fill="#bcd4f0" stroke="#111"/>'+'<g stroke="#111" stroke-width="2.5"><circle cx="160" cy="50" r="12" fill="#2a9d6f"/><path d="M140 140 q-4 -56 20 -72 q24 16 20 72z" fill="#ffd60a"/></g><path d="M190 40 q24 44 0 90" fill="none" stroke="#7a5a3a" stroke-width="5"/>',
 '','<b>Vajramakuṭa.</b> The diamond crown of the Lord at Tirumala, a famous ornament. Then the avatāras: Varāha, Kṛṣṇa who lifted Govardhana, and Rāma, son of Daśaratha, who crushed ten-headed Rāvaṇa, riding Garuḍa, dear to the Pāṇḍavas.','Crowned in diamonds, <b>He is Rāma and Kṛṣṇa too</b>.'),
((7,8),'ALL TEN AVATĀRAS',lambda: ''.join(f'<g transform="translate({22+44*(k%5)} {50+64*(k//5)})"><circle r="18" fill="#fff" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/><text y="5" text-anchor="middle" font-size="16">{e}</text></g>' for k,e in enumerate(['🐟','🐢','🐗','🦁','☂️','🪓','🏹','🌾','🪷','🐎'])),
 '','<b>The daśāvatāra in two stanzas.</b> Fish, Tortoise, Boar, Man-lion, Dwarf, Paraśurāma (7); then Balarāma\'s younger brother Kṛṣṇa, the Buddha and Kalki (8). Unlike the Acyutāṣṭakam\'s list, this one includes the Buddha. Stanza 8 ends with <i>Veṅkaṭaramaṇa</i>, the name in brackets after every refrain.','Every avatāra, <b>gathered on the Seven Hills</b>.'),
((9,10),'FRIEND OF THE HELPLESS',lambda: VIS(140,0,.6)+person(50,150,'#ff8c1a','#f2c79b',.55)+'<path d="M66 106 q30 -14 54 -20" stroke="#3b6fd0" stroke-width="5" fill="none"/>'+lab(60,30,'ĀPADBĀNDHAVA',8),
 '','<b>The refuge names.</b> Sītā\'s husband, who protects and nourishes those who come to Him and establishes dharma; protector of the helpless, <b>friend in trouble</b> (āpadbāndhava), ocean of compassion. These are the names pilgrims shout when they climb the hills in hope.','He is <b>our friend in trouble</b>.'),
((11,12),'"PROTECT US, MURĀRI!"',lambda: VIS(110,0,.6)+'<path d="M20 30 q10 -10 20 0" fill="none"/>'+SUN(190,40,14)+'<circle cx="104" cy="80" r="5" fill="#e05a8a" stroke="#111"/>'+lab(40,150,'PĀHI!',14),
 '','<b>A cry in the middle.</b> Most lines are names, but stanza 11 ends with a plea: <i>pāhi murāre</i>, "protect us, O enemy of Mura!" Stanza 12 names the marks on His chest: Śrī\'s sign and the Śrīvatsa, and calls Him bright as the sun.','In the middle of the names, <b>a cry: "Protect us!"</b>'),
((13,14),'PADMĀVATĪ AND THE FEAR-NOT HAND',lambda: VIS(140,0,.6)+'<path d="M166 104 v-20 h14 v20z" fill="#3b6fd0" stroke="#111"/>'+'<g transform="translate(60 0)" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2.5"><circle cy="60" r="12" fill="#f2c79b"/><path d="M-20 140 q20 -60 40 0z" fill="#e05a8a"/></g>'+lab(60,156,'PADMĀVATĪ',8),
 '','<b>Padmāvatī (13).</b> Lakṣmī as Śrīnivāsa\'s bride, honoured at Tiruchanur below the hills. <b>Abhayahasta</b>: His hand raised in the gesture "do not fear". Stanza 14: conch, discus, bow and mace, and the sacred <i>Virajā</i> waters said in temple tradition to flow beneath the shrine.','His hand says: <b>"Do not fear."</b>'),
((15,16),'THOUSAND-NAMED, GARUḌA-BORNE',lambda: VIS(80,0,.55)+'<path d="M150 70 l-30 -20 l30 10 l30 -10 z" fill="#e0a800" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'+'<g transform="translate(170 130)" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"><ellipse rx="26" ry="14" fill="#888"/><path d="M-26 0 q-14 10 -10 24" fill="none" stroke="#888" stroke-width="6"/></g>'+lab(80,150,'SAHASRANĀMA',8),
 '','<b>Gajarājarakṣaka (16).</b> The elephant-king Gajendra, caught by a crocodile, called out to the Lord, who rushed to him on Garuḍa. It is one of the favourite stories of rescue. The musk <i>tilaka</i> and golden robes describe the Lord as He is adorned at Tirumala.','He who rescued the elephant <b>will rescue us</b>.'),
((17,18),'GOD MADE VISIBLE',lambda: '<rect x="0" y="120" width="220" height="40" fill="#4aa3df"/>'+''.join(f'<ellipse cx="{20+28*k}" cy="128" rx="12" ry="5" fill="#c9a26b" stroke="#111"/>' for k in range(8))+VIS(110,0,.5)+''.join(f'<circle cx="{40+30*k}" cy="{112}" r="6" fill="#8a6a4a" stroke="#111"/>' for k in range(2))+lab(110,24,'PRATYAKṢADEVA',9),
 '','<b>Pratyakṣadeva (18).</b> "God you can see": the Lord of Tirumala is often called <i>Kaliyuga pratyakṣa daivam</i>, the God who shows Himself in this age. Stanza 17 recalls Rāma: served by monkeys, He bridged the ocean to Laṅkā; one form, Rāma and Kṛṣṇa.','The God <b>who lets Himself be seen</b>.'),
((19,20),'THE INTEREST COLLECTOR',lambda: VIS(140,0,.55)+''.join(f'<circle cx="{40+14*k}" cy="{120-8*(k%2)}" r="7" fill="#ffd60a" stroke="#111"/>' for k in range(5))+'<path d="M30 140 h70 v10 h-70z" fill="#c9a26b" stroke="#111"/>'+lab(60,30,'VAḌḌIKĀSULAVĀḌĀ',8),
 '','<b>A Telugu name with a story.</b> <i>Vaḍḍikāsulavāḍā</i>, "the one who collects the interest". Temple legend tells that Śrīnivāsa borrowed wealth from Kubera for His wedding to Padmāvatī, and devotees\' offerings in the hundi help repay the interest till the end of the age. A loving joke between the Lord and His devotees.','Our offerings <b>help the Lord pay His wedding debt</b>.'),
((21,22),'SELF-LUMINOUS, ON OUR SIDE',lambda: SUN(110,60,30).replace('#ffd60a','#fff8c0')+VIS(110,10,.45)+crowd(['#ff8c1a','#e05a8a'],40,140,150,.4),
 '','<b>Āśritapakṣa (22).</b> "Who takes the side of those who come to Him": the Lord is not neutral about His devotees. He is self-luminous (21), needing no other light, and the abode of bliss: the <i>Ānanda Nilaya</i> is also the name of the gilded tower over His shrine.','He is <b>on the side of all who come to Him</b>.'),
((23,24),'RESCUER OF THE ELEPHANT, RESTING ON ŚEṢA',lambda: '<rect x="0" y="120" width="220" height="40" fill="#4aa3df"/><path d="M30 116 q80 -24 160 0" fill="#e0e0e0" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M60 108 q60 -14 100 -4" fill="none" stroke="#3b6fd0" stroke-width="9"/>'+'<g transform="translate(60 60)" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"><ellipse rx="22" ry="12" fill="#888"/></g>',
 '','<b>Here and hereafter (23).</b> <i>Ihaparadāyaka</i>: He gives what we need in this life and the next. <i>Ibharājarakṣaka</i>, rescuer of the elephant-king, returns. Then (24) the Lord reclining on Śeṣa, and living on Śeṣādri, the hill named after the serpent (see Śāntākāram).','Giver of this world <b>and the next</b>.'),
((25,26),'THE AVATĀRAS AGAIN',lambda: ''.join(f'<g transform="translate({30+40*k} 90)"><circle r="16" fill="#fff" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/><text y="5" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14">{e}</text></g>' for k,e in enumerate(['🐗','🐢','☂️','🦁','🪓','🏹'])),
 '','<b>Rūpa: forms.</b> Boar, Tortoise, Dwarf and Man-lion as "forms" (<i>rūpa</i>) of the one Lord; then the three Rāmas: Paraśurāma, Balarāma and Rāma of Raghu\'s line, and Rāma-Kṛṣṇa together. Repetition is the heart of nāma-saṅkīrtana: the names come round again like a wheel.','The names come round again, <b>like a turning wheel</b>.'),
((27,28),'GUARDIAN OF THE HOLY HILL',lambda: HILL+'<g stroke="#111" stroke-width="2.5"><rect x="140" y="50" width="40" height="40" fill="#ffd60a"/><path d="M134 50 l26 -30 l26 30z" fill="#e0a800"/></g>'+lab(60,30,'KṢETRAPĀLAKA',9),
 '','<b>Govindanāma (28).</b> "Whose name is Govinda": the chant names its own heart. <b>Kṣetrapālaka</b>: guardian of the sacred place. Tirumala\'s Lord protects not just His devotees but His whole holy hill.','His very name <b>is Govinda</b>.'),
((29,30),'LORD OF THE SEVEN HILLS',lambda: ''.join(f'<path d="M{8+30*k} 140 l15 -{40+(k%3)*14} l15 {40+(k%3)*14}z" fill="#2a7a4a" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>' for k in range(7))+lab(110,26,'ĒḌUKOṆḌALAVĀḌĀ',10),
 '','<b>Ēḍukoṇḍalavāḍā (29).</b> Telugu for "the one of the seven hills": the Sanskrit Saptagirīśa in the people\'s own language. Pilgrims call out this name as they climb. <b>Ēkatvarūpa</b>: one in form, though worshipped in many names.','Ēḍukoṇḍalavāḍā: <b>the Lord of the Seven Hills</b>.'),
((31,32),'ŚIVA AND KEŚAVA IN ONE',lambda: VIS(80,0,.55)+'<g transform="translate(160 40)" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"><path d="M0 0 q-10 20 0 40 q10 -20 0 -40z" fill="#2a9d6f"/><path d="M-12 8 q-10 18 4 30 M12 8 q10 18 -4 30" fill="none" stroke="#2a9d6f" stroke-width="3"/></g>'+lab(160,100,'BILVA',9),
 '','<b>A surprising stanza (32).</b> Worshipped with <i>bilva</i> leaves, usually offered to Śiva; "female and male in form"; <i>Śivakeśavamūrti</i>, Śiva and Keśava in one. The chant embraces the whole divine. Stanza 31 repeats the diamond armour, the Vaijayantī garland and the interest collector.','Bilva leaves for Viṣṇu: <b>Śiva and Keśava are one</b>.'),
((33,34),'DEAR TO HĀTĪRĀMA',lambda: VIS(140,0,.55)+person(60,150,'#ff8c1a','#f2c79b',.6)+''.join(f'<rect x="{30+12*k}" y="{100}" width="10" height="10" fill="#fff" stroke="#111"/>' for k in range(3))+lab(60,30,'HĀTĪRĀMA',10),
 '','<b>Hātīrāmapriya (33).</b> Tirumala legend tells of Hātīrām Bāvājī, a saint from the north who played dice with the Lord Himself. A name that keeps a devotee\'s story alive. <b>Abhiṣekapriya (34)</b>: the Lord who loves His sacred bath, part of His weekly worship at Tirumala.','The Lord who played dice <b>with His devotee</b>.'),
((35,36),'PRAISED BY RĀMĀNUJA',lambda: person(60,150,'#ff8c1a','#f2c79b',.7)+VIS(150,0,.55)+'<path d="M76 100 q30 -20 50 -20" fill="none" stroke="#111" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/>'+lab(60,30,'RĀMĀNUJA',10),
 '','<b>Rāmānujanuta (35).</b> "Praised by Rāmānuja": the great Śrīvaiṣṇava teacher is remembered at Tirumala for his role in its worship. The chant honours him in a single word. Stanza 36: Lord of all worlds, bliss in form, without beginning or end.','<b>The teacher who praised Him</b> is remembered too.'),
((37,38),'BACK TO ŚRĪNIVĀSA',lambda: VIS(110,0,.75)+'<path d="M84 70 q26 40 52 0" fill="none" stroke="#2a9d6f" stroke-width="5"/>'+''.join(f'<circle cx="{88+8*k}" cy="{82+(k%2)*4}" r="2.5" fill="#2a7a4a"/>' for k in range(6))+lab(110,156,'ŚRĪ ŚRĪNIVĀSA',10),
 '','<b>The circle closes.</b> The last stanza adds the <i>tulasī</i> garland, the Lord\'s favourite, and the serpent-hill once more, then returns to the very first two names: <i>Śrī Śrīnivāsa, Śrī Veṅkaṭeśa</i>. Ending where it began, the chant can go round again, as pilgrims sing it climbing the hills.','It ends where it began: <b>Śrī Śrīnivāsa, Govindā</b>.'),
]
